From 5594a23e464c27b24aa5fbfd91520c14458f134a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Eduardo=20Garc=C3=ADa-M=C3=A1dico=20Portabella?=
 <woodyst@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 18:30:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] media: qcom: camss: vfe: arm the interrupt before resetting,
 not after

Patch 'keep the interrupt disabled until the block is powered' fixed the
crash at module load, but put enable_irq() *after* vfe_reset(). vfe_reset()
waits for a reset-done interrupt, so with the line still disabled it could
only ever time out: every VIDIOC_STREAMON failed with -EIO and

  qcom-camss ace0000.camss: VFE reset timeout
  qcom-camss ace0000.camss: Failed to power up pipeline: -5

The module load was verified back then, but not a capture, so this went
unnoticed.

Move enable_irq() before the reset. That still keeps what the disabled line
was protecting - the power domain is on, pm_runtime is resumed and the
clocks are running, so the handler can safely touch VFE registers - and it
matches the vendor driver, which subscribes the reset interrupt right
before issuing the reset and unsubscribes it afterwards (cam_vfe_reset(),
cam_vfe_core.c).

Also disable the line again on the error path, so a failed reset does not
leave it enabled while the clocks go away.

Verified on a POCO X3 NFC (SM7150): the pipeline powers up, CSID reports
its version (2.0.0), link validation passes and 'vfe irq handled!' appears.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-vfe.c | 22 ++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-vfe.c b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-vfe.c
index dcd17df25..f9def765f 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-vfe.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-vfe.c
@@ -1111,6 +1111,23 @@ int vfe_get(struct vfe_device *vfe)
 		if (ret < 0)
 			goto error_pm_runtime_get;
 
+		/*
+		 * Arm the interrupt BEFORE resetting. vfe_reset() waits for a
+		 * reset-done interrupt, so with the line still disabled it can
+		 * only ever time out - which is exactly what happened: every
+		 * VIDIOC_STREAMON failed with -EIO and "VFE reset timeout".
+		 *
+		 * Doing it here is still safe, and keeps what the disabled line
+		 * was protecting: the power domain is on, pm_runtime is resumed
+		 * and the clocks are running, so the handler can touch VFE
+		 * registers.
+		 *
+		 * This is also what the vendor driver does: it subscribes the
+		 * reset interrupt right before issuing the reset and
+		 * unsubscribes it afterwards (cam_vfe_reset(), cam_vfe_core.c).
+		 */
+		enable_irq(vfe->irq);
+
 		ret = vfe_reset(vfe);
 		if (ret < 0)
 			goto error_reset;
@@ -1120,9 +1137,6 @@ int vfe_get(struct vfe_device *vfe)
 		vfe_init_outputs(vfe);
 
 		vfe->res->hw_ops->hw_version(vfe);
-
-		/* Safe now: clocks are running and the block has been reset. */
-		enable_irq(vfe->irq);
 	} else {
 		ret = vfe_check_clock_rates(vfe);
 		if (ret < 0)
@@ -1135,6 +1149,8 @@ int vfe_get(struct vfe_device *vfe)
 	return 0;
 
 error_reset:
+	/* Undo the enable_irq() above before the clocks go away. */
+	disable_irq(vfe->irq);
 	camss_disable_clocks(vfe->nclocks, vfe->clock);
 
 error_pm_runtime_get:
-- 
2.43.0

